There’s nothing more frustrating than thinking you’ve struck gold after buying a certified used car, only to find a series of hidden issues.

Consumers often believe they’re guaranteed to get a high-quality vehicle if it’s labeled ‘certified pre-owned’. Unfortunately, however, many of them can have serious safety defects.

The best defense against certified pre-owned car scams is knowing how to spot them. In this guide, we’ll walk you through some of the most popular forms of fraud and offer tips on how to avoid them. Once you learn these common duping tactics, you should be able to dodge shady dealers and avoid bad deals.

But first, let’s understand what a certified pre-owned vehicle is.

## Understanding Certified Pre-Owned Vehicles

A used car, truck, or SUV can be listed as [certified pre-owned (CPO)](https://www.nerdwallet.com/article/loans/auto-loans/certified-preowned-cars) if it meets certain quality standards set by the original manufacturer. Different automakers can have different criteria, but a few of the most common ones are as follows.

- CPO vehicles usually must be five to seven years old and have fewer than 60,000 to 90,000 miles.

- The vehicle must not have any frame damage or damage caused by flood, collision, or fire.

- The vehicle should not be a lemon law buyback

- The odometer of the vehicle must clearly show its actual mileage

- All mechanicals and systems need to be in proper working order.

A major aspect of CPO programs is a rigorous and detailed inspection. This generally includes a checklist of 100 to 200 items, where everything from the engine components to the upholstery is examined. Only vehicles that pass this inspection are certified.

## Benefits of Owning a Certified Pre-Owned Vehicle

Considering a certified pre-owned (CPO) vehicle? Here are some great reasons why it might be the perfect fit for you.

- Peace of Mind with Extended Warranty: One of the top perks of CPO vehicles is the extended warranty coverage. It’s longer than what you’d get with a new car, covering major parts and sometimes even offering full bumper-to-bumper protection.

- Quality Assurance from Thorough Inspection: As mentioned, each CPO vehicle undergoes a detailed inspection by certified technicians. This means fewer surprises and fewer out-of-pocket repair costs for you.

- Save Money on a Nearly-New Car: CPO cars come with the latest features; but are much more affordable and don’t depreciate as quickly after you drive off the lot.

- Backed by the Manufacturer: When you buy a CPO car, you get support straight from the manufacturer, including perks like roadside assistance, loaner cars, and even free oil changes.

- Keep Its Value Longer: CPO cars hold their value better than average used cars. Thanks to the rigorous standards they meet, they’re a smarter buy if you’re thinking about future resale.

## Certified Pre-Owned Car Scams You Should Be Aware Of

Shopping for a certified vehicle can feel like an ideal bet. But even in the seemingly safe world of CPOs, you need to keep your eyes open for scams.

Here are some certified pre-owned vehicle scams every buyer should watch out for.

## 1. Fake Certification Labels

Some dealers may slap a ‘certified’ label on a car without it being certified by the manufacturer. Hence, it’s important to verify the certification directly with the manufacturer’s website or by calling their hotline. If the certification cannot be confirmed, you may be looking at a certified car scam.

## 2. Unrevealed Accident History

Just because a vehicle is certified, does not mean it’s accident-free. Some dishonest dealers might not disclose the full history of the vehicle’s accidents and repairs. Always check the vehicle safety report yourself for any hidden past. CPO cars must have a clean, non-branded title to truly hold up to their certified status.

## 3. Selling Lemons

Sometimes, vehicles that were bought back under California lemon law are cleaned up and resold as CPOs without any declaration of their history. To avoid buying a repackaged lemon, be sure to look deeper into the vehicle’s [repair history](/blog/how-to-know-vehicle-lemon-history) and insist on a comprehensive disclosure.

## 4. Warranty Issues

Some dealers in California could promise you a manufacturer-backed [warranty](/blog/what-voids-car-warranty), but end up offering a lesser, third-party warranty that covers much less than expected. Remember to get the warranty details in writing and confirm that it is indeed backed by the manufacturer, not just an add-on that the dealer profits from.

## 5. Double Charging on Features

In some certified car scams, dealers may double-charge you for features or add-ons that are supposed to be included in the price of a CPO vehicle. You can prevent this by comparing the vehicle’s price and included features against the manufacturer’s specifications for CPO vehicles.

## 6. Tampered Odometer

This old trick still makes its rounds in the CPO market. Lower mileage can make a vehicle qualify for CPO status when it shouldn’t. Cross-reference the mileage on the odometer with the vehicle history report/service records for any discrepancies.

